# Suppression and Enrichment

## Fixed maintenance windows

A silence owns one UUID, including the entire team set. Team URLs are views of
that same object. Supply fixed finite bounds; expiration is observed state and
leaves the resource in Terraform state.

```terraform
resource "onpager_silence" "maintenance" {
  scope      = "team"
  team_ids   = [onpager_team.operations.id]
  expression = "labels.service == \"api\""
  starts_at  = "2030-01-01T09:00:00+03:00"
  ends_at    = "2030-01-01T10:00:00+03:00"
  comment    = "Planned API maintenance"
}
```

Tenant scope requires an empty team set and the separate global grants. Team
scope needs write authority across every selected team. A cancelled silence is
retained but cannot be edited/restored; explicitly replace it when a new silence
is intended. Destroy deletes the whole configuration. Historical snapshots keep
the anonymous suppression fact with the API's selected redaction of deleted
configuration details. Deletion does not replay suppressed work.

Local datetime input uses optional provider `timezone`; explicit offsets retain
absolute instants. The backend resolves local input, including DST errors. The
provider preserves microsecond precision and detects externally changed instants.
No relative duration or automatic current-time expression is needed.

## Dependent alert suppression

An inhibition rule owns source/target conditions and its complete matcher set.
Matcher keys are literal label names, including dots or slashes. Use explicit
`resolved_target_action` to select `release` or `keep` behavior.

```terraform
resource "onpager_inhibition_rule" "cluster" {
  name                   = "Cluster suppresses secondary alerts"
  source_expression      = "labels.alertname == \"ClusterUnavailable\""
  target_expression      = "labels.service == \"api\""
  matchers               = [{ source = "cluster", target = "cluster" }]
  resolved_target_action = "release"
  enabled                = false
}
```

Disabled creation stores the complete inactive definition atomically. The index
state is observed runtime metadata, independent of the saved configuration CAS.
Destroy deletes the current configuration while preserving referenced immutable
versions and saved hold/release/keep decisions. It does not force-release holds.

## Dataset content and rule ownership

A dataset owns its metadata and canonical set of string-map rows. Row order and
duplicates do not define separate desired data. The name is immutable; metadata
and row publication share `configuration_version`. Disabled creation and content
updates preserve disabled state. Row reads need the separate sensitive native
grant, and configured rows persist in Terraform state.

```terraform
resource "onpager_enrichment_dataset" "owners" {
  name        = "service-owners"
  description = "Service ownership"
  enabled     = true
  rows = [
    { service = "api", owner = "platform" },
    { service = "billing", owner = "payments" },
  ]
}

resource "onpager_enrichment_rules" "tenant" {
  rules = [{
    id         = "11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111"
    dataset_id = onpager_enrichment_dataset.owners.id
    lookup     = [{ service = "labels.service" }]
    emit       = { owner = "owner" }
  }]
}
```

Exactly one rules resource owns the complete ordered tenant snapshot. Keep rule
UUIDs stable and preserve lookup alternative order. Create refuses an existing
nonempty snapshot; import `tenant` before deliberately adopting its entire current
configuration. An explicit `rules = []` owns an empty snapshot. Resource destroy
also publishes an empty snapshot, retaining old immutable revisions.

Dataset destroy uses DELETE with its saved configuration version. Current rules
can block deletion with `dataset_in_use`; remove their references explicitly.
Referenced historical revisions/results and credential authorship remain intact;
unreferenced data may be pruned. Resource operations do not issue publisher keys
or submit events. Configuring these rules affects subsequent backend processing.

## Errors and permissions

Use each resource page's explicit native read/write/delete grants; account or
team ownership alone grants no business access. Both PATs and native service
accounts are supported. Missing authority, conflicts and transport errors retain
state. Silence/inhibition use strong numeric If-Match; enrichment uses body
expected versions/revisions. Run a new plan after a conflict rather than forcing
an overwrite. A committed partial update remains in state if a later request fails.
